C o m p i l a t i o n


      ****************************
      CEE 6410 - Water Resources Systems Analysis
      HW 4 – More Complicated Linear Programs
      Reservoir Operation Problem
      ****************************
       
      THE PROBLEM:
      A reservoir is designed to provide hydropower and water for irrigation.
      The turbine releases may also be used for irrigation as shown in Figure 1.
      At least one unit of water must be kept in the river each month at point A.
      The hydropower turbines have a capacity of 4 units of water per month (flows are constant during any single month), and any other releases must bypass the tur¬bines.
      The size of farmed area is very large relative to the amount of irrigation water available, so there is no upper limit on usable irrigation water.
      The reservoir has a capacity of 9 units, and initial storage is 5 units of water. The ending storage must be equal to or greater than the begin¬ning storage.
      The benefits per unit of water, and the estimated average inflows to the reservoir are given in Table 1.
       
      Table 1
      Month   Inflow Units    Hydropower Benefits ($/unit)    Irrigation Benefits ($/unit)
      1       2               1.6                             1.0
      2       2               1.7                             1.2
      3       3               1.8                             1.9
      4       4               1.9                             2.0
      5       3               2.0                             2.2
      6       2               2.0                             2.2
       
       
      A.  Develop and solve an LP model for maximizing the economic benefits of reservoir opera¬tion.
       
      THE SOLUTION:
      Uses General Algebraic Modeling System to Solve this Linear Program
       
      Talha Quddoos
      talha.quddoos@usu.edu
      October 05, 2026
  36   
  37  * 1. DEFINE the SETS
  38  * Defining a set for time
  39  SETS
  40      t months /1, 2,3,4,5,6/
  41   
  42   
  43   
  44  * 2. * Setting up dataset
  45   
  46  PARAMETERS
  47      riverInflow      River Inflow /1/
  48      turbineCapacity   Turbine Capacity /4/
  49      resviorCapacity    Capacity of Reservior /9/
  50      ResviorStore       Initial Reservior Storage /5/;
  51   
  52   
  53  * Input Data Table
  54   
  55      table data(t,*)
  56                  InflowUnits HydroBenefit       IrrigationBenefit
  57          1       2           1.6                1.0
  58          2       2           1.7                1.2
  59          3       3           1.8                1.9
  60          4       4           1.9                2.0
  61          5       3           2.0                2.2
  62          6       2           2.0                2.2;
  63   
  64   
  65  * 3. DEFINE the variables
  66  VARIABLES
  67      s(t)      Storage unit at end of each month
  68      wTr(t)    Water unit used by turbine each month
  69      spill(t)  Water units spilled from reservoir bypassing hydropower turbines in each month
  70      wIr(t)    Water units used for Irrigation in each month
  71      flowA(t) Water units retained in River at A each month
  72      B         Total benefits;
  73   
  74  * Non-negativity constraints
  75  POSITIVE VARIABLES S, wTr, spill, wIr, flowA;
  76   
  77  * 4. COMBINE variables and data in equations
  78  EQUATIONS
  79  objective            equation representing objective function
  80  balance1             mass balance eq for 1st month
  81  resMassBalance(t)    water mass balance for month 2 to 6
  82  resCapacity(t)       reservoir capacity constraint
  83  turbCapacity(t)      turbine capacity constraint
  84  irrDiversion(t)      diversion to irrigation
  85  minFlowA(t)          minimum flow constraint at A
  86  sStartEnd            ending storage at least equal to initial storage;
  87   
  88  * Objective Function
  89  objective..  B =e= sum(t, data(t,'HydroBenefit')*wTr(t)) + sum(t, data(t,'IrrigationBenefit')*wIr(t));
  90   
  91  * Mass balance for month 1, starting from the initial storage
  92  balance1.. s('1') =e= ResviorStore + data('1','InflowUnits') - spill('1') - wTr('1');
  93   
  94  * Mass balance for months 2 to 6, starting from the previous month's storage
  95  resMassBalance(t)$(ord(t) > 1).. s(t) =e= s(t-1) + data(t,'InflowUnits') - spill(t) - wTr(t);
  96   
  97  * Reservoir capacity constraint
  98  resCapacity(t).. s(t) =l= resviorCapacity;
  99   
 100  * Turbine capacity constraint
 101  turbCapacity(t).. wTr(t) =l= turbineCapacity;
 102   
 103  * Irrigation diversion: turbine release plus spill, minus the flow left at A, goes to irrigation
 104  irrDiversion(t).. wTr(t) + spill(t) - flowA(t) =e= wIr(t);
 105   
 106  * Minimum flow requirement at point A
 107  minFlowA(t).. flowA(t) =g= riverInflow;
 108   
 109  * Ending storage at least equal to initial storage
 110  sStartEnd.. s('6') =g= ResviorStore;
 111   
 112  * 5. DEFINE the MODEL from the EQUATIONS
 113  MODEL reservoir /all/;
 114   
 115  * 6. SOLVE the MODEL
 116  SOLVE reservoir USING LP MAXIMIZING B;
 117   
 118  * 7. Display results
 119  DISPLAY B.l, wTr.l, spill.l, wIr.l, flowA.l, s.l;


